WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.636
NARRATOR: As the opening day
of the Second World War fades,

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:07.374
Lemp strains to identify
the ship in front of him.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.543
CHRISTIAN JENTZSCH: It's
behaving, in his opinion,

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.813
like an auxiliary cruiser
because it's zig-zagging

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.182
and it's blacked out.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:21.421
And he even imagines to
have seen gun platforms.

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:23.656
NARRATOR: In Lemp's mind,
that is enough proof

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.761
this is some kind of
armed enemy ship fair game

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.262
to be attacked without warning.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:34.300
With torpedoes ready--

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.337
[non-english speech]

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.507
NARRATOR: --a little
before 8:00 PM Lemp

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:44.778
fires the first to U-boat
torpedoes of World War II.

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:48.014
A hydrophone operator
monitors them by the sound

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:50.250
of their propellers.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:52.152
One speeds towards the target.

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.623
But there is a problem
with the second.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:01:00.160
It should grow fainter but
instead is getting louder.

00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:08.001
Fearing that it will circle
back and strike them,

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.271
Lemp orders the U-boat deeper.

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:16.242
It is a scenario they train
for, even in peacetime.

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:19.946
The standard procedure
is to dive below the depth

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.381
that a torpedo would pass.

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.388
NARRATOR: It could be tight.

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:31.624
U-30 is more than 210 feet long.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:35.562
And as the bow angles
down, the stern goes up.

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.798
They must get the whole
U-boat out of danger.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:42.969
They have no way to
visually monitor the threat.

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:46.506
Their only source of information
is what the hydrophone operator

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:47.941
can hear.

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:50.910
[propeller whirling]

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.619
When the noise
finally fades away,

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.855
the torpedo no longer
poses a threat.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:15.301
Relieved, Lemp surfaces
to investigate.

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:19.506
With darkness falling, he
moves in for a closer look.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:23.510
The first torpedo
had struck the ship.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:26.479
But as Lemp surveys
the crippled vessel,

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:29.949
he realizes that
something is wrong.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:33.052
They check the silhouette of the
large ship against the Lloyd's

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.321
Register of vessels.

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:37.290
When he saw the
scene of action,

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:40.126
he realized that
he was mistaken.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.694
It wasn't an auxiliary cruiser.

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:45.632
It was a steamer with a lot
of civilians in the water.

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.800
NARRATOR: An intercepted
radio transmission

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:48.835
confirms his fears.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.974
It identifies the ship
as the passenger liner

00:02:55.041 --> 00:02:58.845
SS Athenia bound for Canada.

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:01.681
Lemp has made a huge mistake.

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.249
His orders were clear.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:07.420
Sink only warships, troop ships,
and enemy merchant vessels.

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:08.688
Hitler and the
German government

00:03:08.755 --> 00:03:11.991
still hope for some
kind of settlement

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:13.760
agreement with Britain.

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:18.631
And so they don't want to
aggravate the British more

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.366
than necessary.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.702
NARRATOR: Now, on the
very first day of the war,

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:25.572
Lemp has sunk a passenger
vessel without warning.

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:33.346
Comparisons are immediately made
to the sinking of RMS Lusitania

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:38.217
by a U-boat in World War I, an
act that helped end American

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:41.888
neutrality and brought the
United States into the war

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:44.390
against imperial Germany--

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.859
the exact scenario
Hitler hoped to avoid

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:49.495
with his strict instructions.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:54.934
Now the sinking of Athenia sets
off a new political firestorm.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:58.137
Many of the 1,103
passengers aboard

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:01.240
are Canadians, Americans,
or European refugees

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:03.710
escaping the war.

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:06.679
118 civilians are dead.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:10.283
Hitler and his propaganda
minister, Joseph Goebbels,

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:15.555
vehemently deny German
responsibility for the attack.

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:17.724
The man responsible
for Hitler's headache

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.629
is 26-year-old [inaudible]
Fritz-Julius Lemp.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.364
CHRISTIAN JENTZSCH:
He was born as a son

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.700
of an army officer in China
before the First World War.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:31.504
He entered the [inaudible]
Marina in 1931.

00:04:31.571 --> 00:04:37.210
And he went to the new
created submarine arm in 1936.

00:04:37.276 --> 00:04:38.444
NARRATOR: He was
assigned command of

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:44.651
his own Submarine U-30 in 1938.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:48.121
Lemp recognizes the potential
fallout from his error.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.423
He makes no radio
report of the sinking

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.125
and continues his patrol.

00:04:52.191 --> 00:04:54.594
He has to keep it secret.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:59.399
And he decides to stick exactly
to the submarine protocol

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:00.466
for his future attacks.

